    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A shop vac advertising 6.5 peak horsepower is plugged into an outlet that can supply about 2.4. The number is not a lie exactly — it is a stalled-motor laboratory measurement — but it tells you nothing about whether the machine will pick up sawdust. These are the numbers that do.
        </p>

        <div id="peak-hp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Peak Horsepower Is a Marketing Number
          </h2>

          <p>
            The largest number on a shop vac box is almost always peak horsepower, and it is the least useful figure printed on the machine. Peak horsepower is measured in a laboratory at the instant the motor is stalled — the moment before it would burn out — with no hose, no filter and no tank restriction.
          </p>

          <p>
            A vac claiming 6.5 peak horsepower is plugged into an ordinary 120V 15-amp household outlet. That circuit can deliver roughly 1,800 watts, which is about 2.4 horsepower before any losses. Six and a half horsepower is not physically available from the socket, and the machine is not producing it.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not a scandal so much as an industry convention, and reputable manufacturers have quietly moved to publishing peak air watts or airflow instead. But the number persists on packaging because it is large, and buyers compare it.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Physics in One Line</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A 15-amp household outlet caps any shop vac at roughly 2.4 theoretical horsepower.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every peak horsepower figure above that describes a stalled-motor laboratory measurement, not the machine you plug into your garage wall. Compare CFM and water lift instead.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="cfm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. CFM: The Number That Moves Debris
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cubic feet per minute measures how much air the machine moves. Airflow is what carries debris up the hose and into the tank, and for most shop vac work it is the specification that predicts performance best.
          </p>

          <p>
            High airflow is what lets a vac pick up wood chips, leaves, gravel and the general bulk of workshop mess. It is also what clears a long hose rather than letting debris settle halfway along it. When a machine feels gutless on chips despite an impressive horsepower claim, low airflow is almost always why.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">CFM Range</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Handles</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Machine</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under 80</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine dust, car interiors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small 2-4 gallon vacs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">80-130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">General garage debris</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid-size 5-9 gallon vacs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">130-180</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wood chips, leaves, water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Larger 12-16 gallon vacs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">180+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Job site and commercial volumes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Contractor machines</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Bear in mind that published CFM is usually measured at the motor with no hose attached. Adding a 2.5-inch hose costs some of it; adding a 1.25-inch hose costs a great deal of it. This is why hose diameter deserves as much attention as the airflow figure itself.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            There is a second reason airflow matters that has nothing to do with pickup: it is what keeps a long hose clear. Debris that enters the nozzle has to stay entrained in the airstream all the way to the tank, and when airflow is marginal, heavier particles drop out and settle in the low points of the hose. You then get the familiar pattern of a machine that picks up for thirty seconds and then goes quiet as a slug of sawdust builds in the corrugations.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is also why hose corrugation and internal smoothness matter. A smooth-bore hose loses less airflow to turbulence than a deeply corrugated one of the same diameter, which is part of why premium extraction hoses cost what they do. On a long run, the difference is easily felt at the nozzle.
          </p>

        <div id="water-lift" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Water Lift: The Number That Pulls
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealed suction, quoted as inches of water lift, measures how hard the machine can pull when the air path is closed off. It is literally how many inches of water the vacuum can raise in a tube.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water lift is what matters when the airflow is restricted — pulling water out of a carpet, extracting through a narrow sander port, or lifting a heavy slug of wet debris. A machine with high airflow and low water lift will move leaves happily and struggle to pull water up a hose. One with high water lift and low airflow will pull hard through a narrow tool port and feel weak on bulk debris.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Water Lift</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Suits</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">40-60 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Light dry pickup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget machines</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">60-80 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">General household and garage use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The practical sweet spot</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">80-100 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wet extraction, tool extraction</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Professional machines</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100+ in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Carpet extraction, commercial</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Specialist equipment</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Between the two figures, the rule of thumb is straightforward: bulk debris and long hoses want CFM; water, carpets and restricted tool ports want water lift. Machines that publish both are generally the ones confident enough in their numbers to invite comparison.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="air-watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Air Watts: The Combined Figure
          </h2>

          <p>
            Air watts combine airflow and suction into a single number, which is why serious manufacturers increasingly quote it. It describes the actual work the machine can do on air, rather than either half of the equation in isolation.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough guide, 200 air watts is modest, 350 is solid for household use, and 500 or more is professional territory. The figure is far more honest than peak horsepower, but it is still measured under standard conditions rather than with your hose, your filter and half a tank of sawdust.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical reality is that all three numbers — CFM, water lift and air watts — fall as the filter loads. A clean machine and a blinded one can differ by more than half, which means filter maintenance moves performance further than choosing between two similar models ever will.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            Air watts also explain why two machines that feel similar in a shop can behave differently on a job. A vac with high airflow and modest sealed suction copes beautifully with an open floor nozzle and struggles the moment you fit a sander shroud, because that restriction is exactly where airflow collapses and suction has to carry the work. The reverse machine feels underwhelming sweeping up chips and excels at tool extraction.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only ever compare one figure, make it air watts, and if a manufacturer does not publish it, make it CFM measured at the hose rather than at the motor. Very few publish the latter, which is itself informative — measuring at the hose is more honest and produces smaller numbers, so the companies that do it are the ones not relying on the number to sell the machine.
          </p>

        <div id="what-changes-in-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Actually Changes Performance in Your Garage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spec sheets describe a new machine in laboratory conditions. These are the factors that decide what you experience.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Filter Condition</h3>
          <p>
            A cartridge loaded with fine dust can halve airflow. This is the single largest variable in day-to-day performance, and it is why a bag matters — it keeps the bulk of the dust off the pleats. Tap or clean the filter between sessions and the machine feels new again.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hose Diameter and Length</h3>
          <p>
            Every foot of hose and every reduction in diameter costs airflow. A 2.5-inch hose at seven feet performs very differently from a 1.25-inch hose at twenty. If you need reach, reach with the wider hose and add an extension rather than switching to a narrow one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tank Fill Level</h3>
          <p>
            A tank full of debris reduces the volume the machine can pull through and, on wet pickup, raises the risk of the float triggering early. Empty it more often than feels necessary, particularly on fine dust.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leaks at the Connections</h3>
          <p>
            An ill-fitting hose cuff or a cracked elbow bleeds air into the system and reduces what reaches the nozzle. If a vac suddenly feels weak and the filter is clean, check the fittings and the tank gasket before blaming the motor.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="comparing-honestly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Compare Two Machines Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Faced with two vacs on a shelf, ignore the horsepower entirely and work through this order.
          </p>

          <p>
            First, look for published CFM and water lift. A manufacturer that prints both is generally telling you something real; one that prints only peak horsepower is generally not. Second, check hose diameter, because a strong motor behind a narrow hose delivers a weak machine. Third, check filter area and whether fine dust filters and bags are available — a machine with no bag option is a poor choice for indoor work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at the practical features that spec sheets leave out: a drain port, a tool-triggered outlet, on-board accessory storage, castors that actually swivel, and a cord long enough to reach across a garage. These decide whether you enjoy using the machine, which in turn decides whether you use it at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, weigh noise. Shop vacs run from about 75 to 90 dB, and the quiet ones are noticeably more pleasant to live with. The difference between 78 and 88 dB is the difference between a machine you will run while someone else is home and one you will not.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            One more real-world factor worth naming: altitude and voltage. A vac running on a long, undersized extension cord sees a voltage drop at the motor, and the airflow falls with it. If a machine seems weak at the far end of a garage on a 50-foot cord, try it plugged directly into the wall before concluding anything about the specification. A 12-gauge cord is the right choice for any shop vac at distance; a thin 16-gauge one costs real performance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Similarly, the exhaust matters. Shop vacs blow their cooling and working air out through a port, and a machine positioned so that exhaust blows directly across a fine dust pile will re-suspend more than it collects. Point the exhaust away from the work, or fit a diffuser if your model has one available — it makes a visible difference on drywall and ash cleanup.
          </p>

        <div id="what-to-ignore" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Specifications You Can Safely Ignore
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Peak horsepower.</strong> Covered above: it is a stalled-motor laboratory figure that exceeds what a household circuit can supply. Two machines with identical peak horsepower claims routinely differ by a third in actual airflow.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tank gallons as a proxy for power.</strong> Capacity tells you how often you empty. It has no bearing on suction or airflow, and large tanks are frequently paired with modest motors at the budget end of the market.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Motor amps in isolation.</strong> Amperage tells you what the motor draws, not what it delivers. An inefficient motor drawing 12 amps can move less air than an efficient one drawing 10. It is a useful sanity check against inflated horsepower claims and nothing more.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Attachment count.</strong> A dozen plastic nozzles in the box is not a feature if nine of them never come out of the drawer. Four good heads — floor brush, crevice, wide utility nozzle and a car detail tip — cover everything most people do, and they are cheap to buy separately if the machine you want ships light.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Machines on Peak Horsepower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is a stalled-motor laboratory number that exceeds what a household circuit can physically supply, and two vacs with identical claims often differ by a third in real airflow. If a manufacturer publishes only horsepower, treat that as information about the manufacturer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming High CFM Fixes Everything</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Airflow moves bulk debris, but restricted jobs — pulling water from carpet, extracting through a sander port — depend on sealed suction. A machine with big airflow numbers and modest water lift will disappoint on exactly those tasks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choking a Strong Motor With a Narrow Hose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Published airflow is measured with no hose attached. Putting a 1.25-inch hose on a powerful vac throws away much of what you paid for. Use the widest hose the job allows and reduce only at the nozzle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the Motor for a Blinded Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A loaded cartridge is the most common cause of a shop vac that 'lost power', and it can halve airflow without looking especially dirty. Clean or replace the filter and use a collection bag before concluding the machine is failing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Air Leaks at the Fittings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hose cuff that no longer seals, a cracked elbow or a perished tank gasket bleeds air into the system, and every cubic foot entering there is one that never reaches the nozzle. Check the connections whenever suction drops suddenly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is peak horsepower on a shop vac real?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any useful sense. Peak horsepower is measured at the moment the motor stalls, with no hose, filter or tank restriction. A standard 120V 15-amp outlet can only supply about 2.4 horsepower in theory, so any claim above that describes a laboratory condition rather than the machine you plug in. Compare CFM and water lift instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is more important, CFM or water lift?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the job. CFM — airflow — is what carries bulk debris such as wood chips, leaves and general mess up the hose, and it matters most for everyday cleanup. Water lift is sealed suction, and it matters when the air path is restricted: pulling water out of carpet, or extracting through a narrow power tool port.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good CFM for a shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Eighty to 130 CFM covers general garage and household work. Under 80 suits fine dust and car interiors only. Above 130 handles wood chips, leaves and water comfortably, and 180 or more is job-site territory. Remember published figures are measured without a hose, so a narrow hose will cost a significant share of it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good water lift figure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sixty to 80 inches of water lift is the practical sweet spot for household and garage use. Forty to 60 is budget territory and will feel weak pulling water. Eighty to 100 inches suits wet extraction and power tool dust collection, where the air path is restricted and sealed suction matters more than raw airflow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are air watts on a vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Air watts combine airflow and suction into one figure representing the work the machine does on air, which makes it more honest than either number alone and far more honest than peak horsepower. Roughly, 200 air watts is modest, 350 is solid for home use, and 500-plus is professional. It is still a clean-filter laboratory figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my shop vac seem weaker than its specs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always the filter. A cartridge loaded with fine dust can halve airflow, and it happens faster than people expect on sanding or drywall work. After that, check for a partially blocked hose, a full tank, a loose hose cuff or a perished tank gasket — air leaking into the system never reaches the nozzle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do more amps mean a better shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably. Amperage describes what the motor draws, not what it delivers, and an inefficient 12-amp motor can move less air than an efficient 10-amp one. It is a useful sanity check against inflated horsepower claims, but airflow, water lift and air watts are the figures that describe performance.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

